# Headers for responses served directly by Cloudflare's static-asset handling,
# which never reaches the Worker — so `headers()` in next.config.ts does not
# apply to them. Confirmed against a local `wrangler dev`: an engine asset came
# back with Cloudflare's default `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` and no security headers, while an HTML page (which does go
# through the Worker) carried the full set from next.config.ts.
#
# This file is Cloudflare-specific and inert everywhere else: `next dev` and
# `next start` serve it as an ordinary public file and keep using
# next.config.ts's `headers()`, so the two must be kept saying the same thing.
# Wrangler excludes `_headers` from the upload automatically.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# Self-hosted engine assets (DuckDB workers + extensions, pdf.js, MapLibre,
# Material Icons). Mirrors ENGINE_ASSET_CACHE_HEADERS in next.config.ts: these
# filenames aren't content-hashed, so a year + `immutable` would risk pinning a
# stale worker or wasm after a dependency bump. A day of hard caching plus a
# week of stale-while-revalidate is fast on repeat visits and self-healing
# within days. The two .wasm binaries served from R2 don't appear here — they
# never reach the asset handler, and their route sets the same value itself.
/duckdb/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/duckdb-ext-files/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/pdfjs/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/maplibre/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800
